


@@BEGIN: WORKSHOP
@id: humdrumR
@title: Exploring the Fundamentals of Information Theory through Musical Data: An Introductory Workshop in HumdrumR
@presenter: condit-schultz
@slides: 
@abstract:

	<a href="http://www.humdrumR.org">HumdrumR</a> is an R package for analyzing data encoded in the
	<a href="https://www.humdrum.org">humdrum data syntax</a>, a powerful and flexible format for
	musical data with many large datasets available online.
	Inspired by the design of the original humdrum toolkit
	(Huron 1995), humdrumR is designed to be appealing and
	accessible to traditional musicologists while also being
	useful to more experienced computational researchers.
	HumdrumR combines the flexibility and transparency of the
	humdrum syntax with the powerful data analysis tools and
	concise syntax of R, making basic musicological analyses
	(i.e., tallying scale degrees) extremely simple and easy
	while simultaneously affording complex analyses, statistical
	testing, and graphing. HumdrumR also includes a suite of
	tools for conducting analyses based on <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Information_theory">information theory</a>,
	including new (as of 2026) tools for modeling the
	information dynamics of music using the <i>prediction by
	partial matching algorithm</i> (Cleary & Witten, 1984), just
	like the <a href="https://www.marcus-pearce.com/idyom/">IDyOM</a> (Pearce, 2005) system.
	
	This workshop is an introduction to humdrumR, suitable for
	music scholars with little or no experience with either
	humdrum or R. We will focus on the analysis of pitch and
	rhythm data in several well-known humdrum datasets: the <a href="https://github.com/craigsapp/bach-370-chorales">Bach
	370 Chorales</a>, the <a href="https://github.com/ccarh/essen-folksong-collection">Essen folk-song collection</a>, <a href="https://github.com/jcdevaney/TAVERN">TAVERN</a>, and
	<a href="https://github.com/Computational-Cognitive-Musicology-Lab/CoCoPops">CoCoPops</a>. Though we will proceed at the pace of the workshop
	participants and explore any topics/questions of interest to
	them, the focus of the workshop will be the application of
	ideas from <b>information theory</b> to musical data. We will show
	how to compute—and interpret—basic information-theory
	metrics (entropy, mutual information) across and between
	different representations of pitch and rhythm, and how to
	compare these metrics between different (sub)sets of musical
	data, or across time. We will take time to discuss and
	<i>understand</i> what information-theory metrics actually mean,
	and how they can be meaningful tools for musicological
	analysis—particularly but not limited to their connection
	to the perception and cognition of music.
	
	The planned (but flexible) schedule of topics is:
	
	1. Tabulating pitch data in various forms (generic/simple
	pitch, absolute pitch vs scale degrees, etc.).
	
	2. Computing (and interpreting) entropy and information
	theory.
	
	3. Comparing distributions and entropy between
	composers/genres, and across time.
	
	4. Exploring correlations and “mutual information”
	between rhythm and pitch.
	
	5. Simple N-gram tabulation, analyses, and conditional
	entropy.
	
	6. Complex N-gram analysis: Information Dynamics of Music
	(“IDyOM”-type computation).
	
	Workshop participants will need to bring their own
	computers. You do not need to install or download anything
	in advance, as all workshop materials (and a functioning R
	instance) will be running on the cloud (Posit cloud). If you
	<i>wish</i> to install humdrumR or download datasets on your
	machine in advance, detailed instructions will be provided
	in advance of the meeting.

@@END: WORKSHOP






